## Obsidian vault — <OWNER>'s cross-project knowledge base

The org memory lives at `$HOME/<VAULT_NAME>` (one folder per project; conventions in its
**Vault Guide** note).

**Read side — consult before building.** When starting a NEW project, or designing a new
subsystem/integration in an existing one, first read:
- `Home.md` — the project index and the "How these connect" cross-project map
- `Capabilities.md` — reusable building blocks and the shared-resource constraints list

Look for prior art to reuse and for shared resources you must not conflict with. Follow
`[[wikilinks]]` into project notes only as needed — don't bulk-read the vault. Skip this for
routine questions or small fixes.

**Write side — sync after substantive work.** Mirror notable work (new features, live
changes, architecture decisions, status changes) back to the vault per its Vault Guide, and
bump the project overview's `last-synced` frontmatter. New reusable capabilities also get an
entry in `Capabilities.md`.

## Code memory — Graphify
In a repo with `graphify-out/graph.json`, run `graphify query "<question>"` before grepping
raw files; `graphify path "<A>" "<B>"` for relationships; `graphify update .` after code
changes (AST-only, no API cost).

## Agent workforce
If this Mac runs Zulu Assistant, the board is at http://localhost:3210 and cross-agent work
goes through its task ledger (`scripts/task.sh`, rules in AGENT-MESH.md). Anything paid,
outward-facing or destructive escalates to <OWNER> per POLICY.md — never guess.

## Protected paths — never delete during cleanup/disk work
`~/Library/Application Support/selfheal-runner/` and `~/.cache/selfheal-runner/` hold the
scheduled jobs' success MARKERS — state, not cache. Deleting them marks every job "never
succeeded" and triggers catch-up re-runs. Restore from the vault's `.scripts/state-mirror/`.
